==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|rightuous}}, {{l|en|rightwise}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{term|rightuous|lang=en}}, {{term|rightwose|lang=en}}, {{term|rightwos|lang=en}}, {{term|rightwise|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|rightwise|lang=enm}}, {{term|rightwis|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|rihtwīs||righteous, just, right, justifiable}}, corresponding to {{suffix|right|wise|lang=en}} (with assimilation of second element to {{term|-ous}}), or to {{compound|right|wise|t2=way, manner|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|richtwis||righteous|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|rehtwīsic||righteous, just}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|réttvíss||righteous, just|lang=is}}. Compare also {{l|en|thefteous}}, {{l|en|mighteous}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈraɪtʃəs/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-righteous.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪtʃəs|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[free]] from [[sin]] or [[guilt]]
# [[moral]] and [[virtuous]], suggesting [[sanctimonious]]
# [[justified]] [[morally]]
# {{context|slang|US|lang=en}} [[awesome]]

====Derived terms====
* [[righteousness]]
* [[self-righteous]]

